WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.585 --> 00:00:06.418
Oh, God.

00:00:06.418 --> 00:00:09.060
He went downrange in a
bomb suit with a crowbar.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.620
I mean, cool.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.440
We would clown this
movie so much in training

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.450
and just do the most outlandish s---

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.040
because that's exactly what he did.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.960
Hey, everyone, Jay Ly here.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.620
13-year Navy explosive
ordnance disposal, or EOD, vet.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.322
Did four combat deployments,

00:00:24.322 --> 00:00:27.450
three to Afghanistan
and one to East Africa.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.820
Today we'll be looking
at bomb-disposal scenes

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.995
in movies and TV and
judge how real they are.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.210
That thing is apparently
the Cadillac of trolleys.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.127
Can I say 100% that this
wouldn't be entertained?

00:00:46.127 --> 00:00:48.810
No, because it might, but what I do know

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:53.010
is there are probably a dozen other ways

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.510
to attack this device from inside the bus.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:58.800
I mean, something rudimentary

00:00:58.800 --> 00:00:59.970
as getting a stick with some film,

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.890
so we can take an X-ray of the device

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.660
so we can actually see what's going on.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.430
Harry: Jack, I'm going to
need you to look at the wire.

00:01:08.084 --> 00:01:09.150
Harry, it's covered.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.760
He is talking on a radio and/or a phone

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.050
right next to this thing.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.670
It's electronically initiated.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:15.750
They don't know if that energy

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.320
will cause that device to fire or not.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.887
So that whole thing is
not realistic whatsoever.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.753
I can't bypass! It'll fire.

00:01:28.753 --> 00:01:30.870
S---, it's a collapsible circuit.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.330
Yeah, collapsing circuits are real.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.670
There's two or more detonators,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.310
and when you cut one,
the other one will fire.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.870
It's common for us to train to that.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.575
It's very unlikely to encounter that.

00:01:43.575 --> 00:01:45.600
In, well, at least in
Afghanistan, in my experience,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.180
we didn't encounter a lot
of collapsing circuits.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.470
It was mainly pressure
plates, stuff like that.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.330
Very simple circuits.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.840
Officer: Howard Payne,
Atlanta PD bomb squad.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.610
Jack, I want you to get
out of there and sit tight.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:57.840
We're going to go after the source.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.680
No, they should 100% do
something to this device

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.280
and not just solely rely on
the bomber helping them out.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.540
There are tools we have

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.480
to disrupt an IED and a component,

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.340
so it'll disrupt the firing train.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.357
It'll disrupt all the circuitry

00:02:14.357 --> 00:02:16.320
and separate components remotely,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.450
and then we'll go down,
check the disruption,

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.820
and then separate components
further and do our thing.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.186
Jack, I believe he is

00:02:23.186 --> 00:02:26.402
a bomb-disposal officer with the LAPD.

00:02:26.402 --> 00:02:29.100
He should know these
component-recognition things

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.040
and be able to identify
how this device works,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.270
or at least have a good idea of it.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.690
This definitely gets a zero
because he knows better,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:37.830
and he should have taken an action.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.360
He should have brought a
tool down there with him.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.010
He could have taped it right next to it

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.380
and disrupted that
thing right off the bus.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.470
We got under three minutes
left. What do I do first?

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.800
Pressure sensors. I
activated it when I sat down.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.930
If I get up, my car explodes.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.530
This is already hokey because he sat down,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.980
and now it's armed. And there's a timer.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.510
In movies and TV, they often
incorporate these big timers

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.190
where it's counting
down to the detonation.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.152
But in all reality, it's mainly

00:03:09.152 --> 00:03:11.677
a safe-and-arm-type mechanism.

00:03:11.677 --> 00:03:15.420
If I'm a bomb emplacer, I
need to be able emplace it

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.890
and then safely get away.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:17.723
So there's a timer,

00:03:17.723 --> 00:03:19.860
but you're not going to
see this giant clock.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.410
It's like, "You have five
minutes before you die."

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.270
Orange, yellow, green.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.670
Colors don't matter. What
I need you to do is tell me

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.185
which one is hot to the touch.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.180
The warm wire versus cold wires,

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.580
you can make a circuit
with the proper resistance,

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.380
so none of the wires are warm.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.160
So, like, the movie
tropes of the warm wire

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.120
or the colors of the
wires is a total farce.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.005
I can go to Home Depot, Lowe's,
whichever hardware store,

00:03:48.005 --> 00:03:50.460
your hardware store of choice,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:55.080
get any color wire I
want, and put it in here.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.533
Take the small box out of
the kit and turn it on.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.890
That kit probably looked like something

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.390
I would pull tools from

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:05.700
in a special-operations-forces
environment.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.770
From my experience in Afghanistan,

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.290
it was all special operations forces

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.910
or naval special warfare.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.197
We flew in helicopters somewhere, landed,

00:04:15.197 --> 00:04:16.888
and ran to a target, right,

00:04:16.888 --> 00:04:20.490
or walked a great distance
to get to a target.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.040
You really want to limit
what you have on your body.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.690
So, yeah, we're not going
to be carrying around

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.932
briefcases and backpacks
just full of tools.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.920
Just cut it in the middle.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.462
The orange wire between the two clamps.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.760
I don't want to get into
that specifically in detail

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.900
because that is how we
attack certain things.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.310
But yeah, those clamps,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:51.630
they're for a very specific
purpose for multiple detonators,

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:55.350
and you would need another
item that he doesn't have there

00:04:55.350 --> 00:05:00.030
to do diagnostic work
on that type of device.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.100
You would need a little bit more time,

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.117
I mean, a lot more training
than just him saying,

00:05:04.117 --> 00:05:05.550
"Oh, yeah, clamp it on the warm wire

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:06.956
and then cut in between it."

00:05:06.956 --> 00:05:08.190
There's a lot of testing you have to do

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.360
before you can cut a wire.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.880
But yeah, the whole setting,
him sitting down in it,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.610
arming it, and then
having this opportunity

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.040
to render it safe, yeah, you wish.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.756
So yeah, we'll give him a
two. We'll give him a two.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:28.080
How it was set up and how it
was in place, totally legit.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.040
He had a Claymore.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.160
He put it on top of the Grand
Slam bomb, huge British bomb.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.835
Claymores are used all
over. They're already wired.

00:05:34.835 --> 00:05:37.860
They're premade to be
set up as booby traps.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:41.260
That's what they're for. So
he just had the trip wire,

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.200
put something that he thought

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.491
one of these people chasing
him, idiots, would grab.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.660
There are 100% bomb-detection dogs,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.210
and in this clip, those dogs, I believe,

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.730
are going for human detection.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.830
They're going for sniffing out Rambo,

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.160
not necessarily sniff out
explosive hazards in the woods.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.980
And even if they were, those
dogs are running full speed.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.410
That's like essentially
you trying to sniff

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.230
a hot dog in the corner while
sprinting down the hallway,

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.510
like hyperventilating essentially.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:16.827
It's never going to happen.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.610
Would the blast wave be that extended?

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:27.000
I mean, it was drawn
out to have that effect.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.550
The explosive component
in there is torpex.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:34.350
They added aluminum into the
composition of that explosive

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.030
to extend the blast.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:40.320
But with that being said,
that's like a nuclear blast wave

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.660
that is just kind of continuing to go out.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.788
It would be really big and done.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.540
That blast wave would've
killed a lesser man.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:55.650
Not John Rambo.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.260
The explosive component
in there is torpex.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.890
It's about 50% stronger than
TNT. And that's only the blast.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:05.610
Frag goes exponentially
further than blast does.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.620
So yeah, he is definitely within that.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.740
This is probably the most realistic clip

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.190
as far as how things function
and how people think.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.560
Man, I might give "Rambo" an eight.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.148
He's knocked two because of survival.

00:07:18.148 --> 00:07:20.760
That's hokey at best, even for John Rambo.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.266
I got it!

00:07:22.266 --> 00:07:26.370
It's a cylinder cipher! D-U-N-N.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.830
It's my last name. It knows who I am.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.279
If someone is just going to make

00:07:30.279 --> 00:07:32.829
an improvised nuclear
device like this cipher,

00:07:32.829 --> 00:07:33.960
anything is possible.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.670
There's many ways that
nuclear weapons are armed.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:40.380
Can't get into any specifics.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:41.400
There's state-sponsored,

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.140
and then there's non-state-sponsored.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.040
State-sponsored are going to have

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.600
all kinds of different protocols in place,

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.790
safeties, what have you, arming sequences.

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.260
Non-state-sponsored, if someone is just

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:51.900
going to make an improvised nuclear device

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.660
like this cipher, it can be
armed when it hears your voice,

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.580
when my voice, whatever the case may be.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.380
Benji: I speak without a mouth.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:01.800
I fly through the air
without wings. What am I?

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:02.633
An echo.

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:04.352
That's it! That's it, that's it.

00:08:04.352 --> 00:08:07.390
Ugh. I can't. I can't even with this one.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:08.940
This is ... he's talking to it.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.640
How does he know if he
answers this question,

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:12.848
it's not going to go off?

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:18.683
It's empty.

00:08:18.683 --> 00:08:21.330
Apparently it was a mud suck, right?

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.450
A hoax IED or a hoax device.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:28.350
In real life, hoaxes are actually
very common, very common,

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.870
especially in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.610
They were utilized

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:36.420
as intelligence-gathering
devices, essentially.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.370
They'd put it in the road. Totally fake.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:42.907
Just something that would
key up an EOD response,

00:08:42.907 --> 00:08:45.300
and then they would be watching

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.510
and videotaping the whole procedure.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:51.720
Despite all of the craziness
that was in the scene,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.540
using a hoax to gather
intelligence on people

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.090
and to learn more about
them, absolutely true.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.023
We'll give this one a eight,
up there with "Rambo."

00:09:04.691 --> 00:09:05.749
Oh, God.

00:09:05.749 --> 00:09:08.310
We would clown this movie
so much in training.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.960
Like, we would just
pretend we're this dude

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:11.940
and just do the most outlandish s---

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:13.740
because that's exactly what he did.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.300
He's attacking a VBIED,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.150
which is a vehicle-borne
improvised explosive device.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:21.180
Walking up to a VBIED without a tool,

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:23.880
an explosive tool, an
energetic tool, or anything,

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.500
not even a pole line or anything
to prosecute this vehicle,

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:29.430
is just dumb.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:32.160
He went downrange in a
bomb suit with a crowbar.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:33.630
I mean, cool.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:34.920
They would probably send a robot down

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.270
with a tool first, right?

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:38.340
Especially because yeah,
that's a lot of HE.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.560
A VBIED is no joke.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.680
I mean, even the guys on the
wall, his security element,

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.496
they'd probably die if
that thing went off.

00:09:47.749 --> 00:09:49.237
What the hell is he doing?

00:09:51.446 --> 00:09:52.579
Dead.

00:09:53.956 --> 00:09:54.990
Dead.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:58.410
He could have died a half
dozen to a dozen ways

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.810
in this clip alone.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.032
Not to mention the whole movie.

00:10:02.032 --> 00:10:05.310
Kicking it, randomly
opening the trunk by hand,

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:08.040
opening the hood by hand,
turning the windshield wipers on,

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.020
yanking random wires underneath the hood.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.510
Like, any one of those could
have set that thing off.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.940
Yeah, I think he removed his headset.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.043
Please put your headset back on.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:22.260
The most realistic thing in
there was the radio thing.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:24.180
He actually took his headset
off so he could focus

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.310
and get his thought process going.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.530
And then when they asked
him to put it back on,

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:30.330
yeah, that's probably the
response that you'd get

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:31.755
from an EOD tech.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.670
We definitely render safe IEDs by hand.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.845
I've done it,

00:10:44.845 --> 00:10:46.894
and it's not ideal.

00:10:46.894 --> 00:10:48.540
We want to go as remote as possible

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.240
because of the risk of
something going off.

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:54.750
But yeah, he could have done
a lot more with his hands,

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:56.430
like cut that cord,

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.080
detonating cord right out of the gate.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.322
I forgot how many 155s,

00:11:00.322 --> 00:11:02.190
but that's what was in
the trunk of that car

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.200
once he saw the main charge,

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.870
and that's what he should
be going after anyway.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:08.400
Just reckless abandon.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.650
Yeah, it's totally Hollywooded up.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.770
The only realistic thing was
the communication aspect of it.

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:16.752
He gets a one. One out of 10.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:23.760
Three miles underwater,

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.350
the water's crystal clear,
so I mean, that's ...

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:29.940
no, there's no way.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:33.210
My fourth team, I was on a
mine-countermeasure team,

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:34.957
but it was underwater sea mines

00:11:34.957 --> 00:11:37.740
and underwater IEDs and stuff like that.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:39.990
This is supposed to be a nuke

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:42.540
that has fallen into the abyss.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:46.200
It's a big deal when we go
to 200 feet of seawater.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.720
We're definitely not going to
do free ascent to the bottom.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:50.070
There's always descent lines.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.050
We establish a safe area.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.940
Yeah, he doesn't have any
search tools whatsoever.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:58.320
He just has his eyeballs and
a flare, which is insane.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:11:59.520
And that's just the diving aspect,

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:01.316
so we haven't even gotten
to the EOD part of it.

00:12:03.242 --> 00:12:04.075
Hiram: He made it.

00:12:04.075 --> 00:12:05.175
Oh, my God.

00:12:05.175 --> 00:12:07.020
We'll go step by step.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.850
The communication is totally wazoo.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.680
It's three miles underwater.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.913
What RF signal can penetrate

00:12:13.913 --> 00:12:16.179
three miles' worth of water?

00:12:16.179 --> 00:12:19.470
We do have very rudimentary
ways of communicating,

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.540
i.e., a float on the top of the water

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.730
that's connected to us via a
line, and we'll just pull it,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.670
and the certain number of
pulls means a certain thing.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:28.470
So we communicate like that

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:31.140
in a bottom-line-centric environment

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:34.200
where we're taking seismic
and magnetic precautions.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.590
But yeah, nuke, that is a
totally different ballgame.

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:40.773
You have to cut the ground
wire, not the lead wire.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:45.360
Without getting into any super specifics

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:47.640
on nuclear-weapons design,

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:50.220
we would've researched this ordnance item:

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:51.960
this is exactly how you render it safe,

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:53.460
if we want to separate components,

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:54.510
separate the fuse from it.

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:56.340
Sometimes we do that explosively,

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:57.948
sometimes we do it mechanically,

00:12:57.948 --> 00:13:00.848
and then we'd just raise it
up and bring it to the beach

00:13:00.848 --> 00:13:02.220
and exploit it on the surface.

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:05.911
I'd have to give that a big
fat doughnut for realism,

00:13:05.911 --> 00:13:08.852
a zero, because that's not at all

00:13:08.852 --> 00:13:10.713
how nuclear weapons function.

00:13:12.535 --> 00:13:15.630
Here! McIntyre, can you hear me?

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:17.700
I love this show. It's so funny.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.495
Unexploded ordnance, UXO.

00:13:20.495 --> 00:13:24.360
Those are very common because
sometimes fuses just dud out,

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:25.380
and it's still a threat

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:29.460
because we just don't
know the condition of it.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:31.290
Why did it not go off?

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:33.450
Is it a time? Is it a proximity thing?

00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:37.050
Is it a delay that just
hasn't gone all the way yet?

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.480
So, very much a concern,

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:40.890
less so for everyone
else that's behind cover,

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:43.397
but mainly for the guys
that are right on top of it.

00:13:43.397 --> 00:13:45.437
Heartbeat's regular.

00:13:45.437 --> 00:13:47.931
[hissing]

00:13:47.931 --> 00:13:49.569
You spring a leak?

00:13:49.569 --> 00:13:50.660
It stopped ticking.

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:53.760
It is definitely plausible
to use a stethoscope

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:57.390
to examine a suspected clockwork fuse.

00:13:57.390 --> 00:14:00.840
Seems hokey, but yeah,
that is old-school legit.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:01.673
It was a thing.

00:14:01.673 --> 00:14:02.970
For old bombs like that,

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.340
they had clockwork mechanisms in the fuse

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.650
that you could hear ticking down.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:08.970
We don't see that a lot anymore.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.820
That was a lot of training in school,

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:13.036
just to get the exposure to it.

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.770
It's a propaganda bomb!

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:23.100
Leaflets are definitely employed.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:27.991
They were dropped in all over Europe

00:14:27.991 --> 00:14:30.299
during World War II.

00:14:30.299 --> 00:14:34.950
They were employed in, I
mean, North Korea, Vietnam.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:39.030
Leaflets are definitely a
type of psychological warfare

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:43.530
or even just a means to
communicate with a population

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:47.340
that just is confined to an area.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.650
But yeah, I'd give it a
solid seven for realism.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:53.640
Granted, two random people
would not be working this bomb,

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.560
but yeah, so we've gotta
dock it a little bit.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:56.560
I just don't know why they didn't have any

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:58.051
bomb-squad people.

00:14:58.051 --> 00:14:59.430
So, on the side of the timer,

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:00.990
there should be a serial number.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:02.767
That'll tell me which
wire y'all need to cut.

00:15:02.767 --> 00:15:06.060
"Archer" here is showing
an inexperienced person

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.190
being coached through this whole ordeal,

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:10.170
like many other movies and TV shows.

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:13.723
It would be almost impossible
unless it was set up

00:15:13.723 --> 00:15:16.352
exactly how the manufacturer
wanted it to be set up.

00:15:16.352 --> 00:15:18.510
If that's the case, totally doable, right?

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.550
I can walk you through
anything. It's like, OK, cool,

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:22.470
how many wires are there,
what colors are they?

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:23.370
You ready?

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:24.360
Lana: God dammit, Archer!

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:25.410
First letter is B.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:26.243
Bravo.

00:15:26.243 --> 00:15:27.570
Thanks. Second letter is --

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.260
M as in Mike?

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:34.260
Oh, my God, that is so spot
on as far as communications,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:36.843
especially when s---'s hitting the fan.

00:15:36.843 --> 00:15:38.509
Oh, I can relate.

00:15:38.509 --> 00:15:40.980
You have radio frequencies,
you have interference,

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.590
you have distance, you have
other things in the way,

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:45.600
so your signal can be degraded.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.910
Everyone is getting
ramped up in this episode,

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:52.260
but in real life, when stuff is going on

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:56.430
and comms are an issue,
you just really stay calm

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.560
and cool and collected on the radio.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.220
That way, even if it is a weak signal,

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:04.080
you can still hear what's being said.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:06.000
Archer: I'm doing it, God!

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.766
Lana: Oh, my God. Ray!
Ray: What's going on?

00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:09.233
The timer sped up!

00:16:09.233 --> 00:16:12.270
The trope here with the
green wires being cut

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:16.260
and speeding up the
clock is just erroneous

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:18.870
because it makes no sense to
have that feature in there

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:22.110
unless you're trying to
create some type of anxiety,

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:23.370
and in real life, trust me,

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:25.710
if you're faced with a live explosive

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:27.750
that can detonate at any action,

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:31.530
keeping that at bay is already
going to be difficult enough.

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:32.400
So yeah, at the end of the day,

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:34.080
people want their things to go off.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:35.820
So that's how they make them.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:36.960
I'll give them a four.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:41.543
The communication piece, that
was hilariously accurate.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:45.000
Towards the general
direction of the mountain.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.700
The robot rarely finds
what you're looking for.

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:51.030
They're in Helmand, which,
I was also in Helmand.

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:52.440
Helmand Province in Afghanistan.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:54.390
And yeah, robots are awesome.

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:56.190
I didn't see the robot
go down with a tool.

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:57.810
Tool in, tool out, that's what we do.

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.060
That's what's preached
from day one in EOD school.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:02.340
The robot has cutters
on it, it has grippers,

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:04.440
it can separate components,
whatever the case may be.

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:08.070
And so if it did find something,
it could countercharge it

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:09.770
or they could set it down.

00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:14.043
No!

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.220
At the end of the day, that
is what a robot is for.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:19.050
They're expendable devices.

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:20.670
If worst comes to worst,

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:22.470
they find something by functioning it,

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:24.060
it blows up, not a human being.

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:27.990
The entire team was standing
out in front of the vehicles.

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.690
That's totally not what would happen.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.900
They would be inside their
vehicle driving this robot.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:36.660
That way, you have mitigation

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.300
from fragmentation coming at you.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:42.750
At least be behind the
vehicle, for God's sake.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:47.313
I need you to keep your head still.

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:49.163
Don't nod.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:52.950
This is a clothespin switch.

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:57.390
Very old and very rudimentary,
very easy to defeat.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:17:59.700
Nick, the EOD operator,
should have seen that.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.240
He identified that it's
a pull. Cool. Cut it.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:04.950
Just cut it and be done with it.

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.440
But he did the thing of holding his head.

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:09.690
You tell someone that
they're connected to a line

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.550
with a bunch of explosives,
they're going to freak out.

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:13.050
That's one of the rare times

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.180
where you can actually attack a switch.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:16.305
It's a very clear cut.

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:26.340
That could have been
handled way differently,

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:28.320
and then everyone would've survived.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:32.040
It's tough. A lot of EOD
procedures and tactics

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:33.930
are written in blood, just like this.

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:34.770
The survivors are like,

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:36.240
OK, this is exactly what they encountered.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:37.860
Over the radio, this is what was said.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:40.290
Now we have our new set of TTPs

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:41.670
so it doesn't happen again.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.950
I'll give this one of
five. It's got some stuff,

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:48.540
some super-hokey stuff,
but all in all, not bad.

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:49.957
Bombs aren't on the same fire line.

00:18:49.957 --> 00:18:51.630
I have to defuse them individually.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:52.620
How long is that going to take?

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:56.550
I gotta find them and then
dismantle them. 30 minutes.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:57.960
There's just, like, a chunk

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:00.780
of high explosive taped to a pipe.

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:02.670
EOD would see that and,
boom, and make a call,

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:04.320
and everyone would get out of there

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:05.670
except for one other person,

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:07.470
him and one other security person,

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:09.720
and then I'm going to take
immediate action on that device.

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.003
And immediate is immediate.
It's not 30 minutes.

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:19.500
He was using a mirror for whatever reason.

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:22.740
I'm not going to use an inspection mirror

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.380
when I have my eyeballs and I
can see all the way around it.

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:26.820
So yeah, that's kind of hokey.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:30.060
He's got NODs on his helmet,
so that's cumbersome,

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:31.350
especially when he's working on that one

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:33.270
that was at head level.
His NODs could hit it,

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.820
and if there's an anti-move
thing on there, boom.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:37.560
He just functioned it, as designed.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.477
So I would take my helmet
off, so there's nothing,

00:19:40.477 --> 00:19:43.111
no obstructions that could
potentially hit this device.

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:51.780
If he's the only one there,

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:52.710
yeah, that's what he's got to do.

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.300
It's because it's threat assessment

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.830
of what's going to kill me right now.

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:57.750
That's an enemy combatant.

00:19:57.750 --> 00:19:59.850
He's probably going to
kill me if he sees me.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.740
So that needs to be taken care of first.

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:04.915
But yeah, he would have

00:20:04.915 --> 00:20:08.431
at least one shooter with
him a little bit away,

00:20:08.431 --> 00:20:12.720
so when I'm working, I
can be focused on this.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:15.770
They could've classed it up
a little bit, but all good.

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:18.870
Bingo.

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:20.340
He did use ceramic scissors at the end.

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:21.570
I thought that was a nice touch. Granted,

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:24.090
he used ceramic scissors
instead of diagonal cutters,

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:26.250
and typically when wires
are coiled up like that,

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:28.080
there's two of them, and he cut them both.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:29.820
He could have smooshed the two wires

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:33.330
that are conductive
together and set it off.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:34.830
Yeah, there's a lot of hokey stuff.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:37.020
So yeah, it's gotta be low. Low three.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:38.074
John: What are you doing?

00:20:38.074 --> 00:20:39.799
Batman: I can get it out over the bay.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:47.820
The plausibility of having a bomb

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:51.660
that is unable to be rendered safe,

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:54.150
it's all a matter of time, really.

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:58.170
If time is not an object,

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:01.830
then yeah, you can spend
the time and the diligence

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:05.820
and really get it disassembled
to where it's demil'd.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:08.280
So now you have to weigh,

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:12.750
can I render this safe in a
minute and 30 seconds or not?

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:14.910
And the answer's probably no, right?

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:16.470
Because especially with a device

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.630
as sophisticated as
that was supposed to be,

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:20.640
your best case is you have a minute and 30

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.732
to get this as far away
from everyone as possible.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:32.910
Yeah, water has a great tamping effect

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:34.650
because it cannot compress.

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:38.190
It really depends on what
the actual explosive,

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:40.200
the nuclear yield of this bomb was

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:42.150
and how deep it was able to go.

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:45.270
The mission of EOD is protection
of personnel and property.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:49.440
So yeah, getting that device
out in a way, moving it,

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.890
relocating it, even if
it's to your own demise,

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:56.380
is what we would do

00:21:56.380 --> 00:21:59.580
for the sake of protecting
personnel and property.

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:02.100
Is it a nine? Can Batman be a nine?

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:03.240
I'll give it a nine.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:06.456
I like to think his actions were genuine

00:22:06.456 --> 00:22:08.439
of Navy EOD.

00:22:08.439 --> 00:22:11.520
I'm going to have to say my
favorite bomb-disposal scene

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.703
of all time has got to be

00:22:14.703 --> 00:22:18.028
the scene in "MacGruber."

00:22:18.028 --> 00:22:20.280
Totally outlandish, but so funny.

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:22.230
But if I'm going for
something with good sentiment,

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:25.590
then today, we saw that Batman video,

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:27.600
and I think that really captured

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.170
the quintessential mindset of Navy EOD

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:34.650
about eliminating a threat
to civilian population

00:22:34.650 --> 00:22:37.230
and mitigating the risk
and moving it away.

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:38.680
Hey, everyone, thanks for watching.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:41.610
If you thought this video
was the bomb, pun intended,

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.193
go ahead, watch the next video.

